<h2>The application and the record</h2>
<p>The applicant, facing trial for an offence in which two people were shot dead during a robbery, sought ten days of interim bail for his brother&rsquo;s marriage. The State&rsquo;s opposition assembled his history: a regular bail plea pursued up to the Supreme Court and withdrawn there; four prior convictions or cases, spanning Sections 302, 307 and 394/397 IPC and Section 42 of the Prisons Act; and two other trials pending against him.</p>

<h2>What the Court asked, and did not get</h2>
<p>The first question in any occasion-based interim bail is the occasion itself. Here, counsel could not identify which specific rituals required the applicant&rsquo;s presence — the application rested on the fact of a wedding rather than on anything the applicant needed to do at it. With the occasion unparticularised, the applicant&rsquo;s bail history and the seriousness of his involvement in multiple violent cases did the rest. The Court did not find &ldquo;a fit case to grant interim bail&rdquo; and directed the jail superintendent to inform the applicant of the outcome.</p>

<h2>The anatomy of a humanitarian interim bail plea</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>A concrete occasion.</strong> Date-fixed, document-backed: the wedding card, the hospital record, the death certificate. Vague references to &ldquo;ceremonies&rdquo; invite exactly the question that sank this application.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>The applicant&rsquo;s necessary role.</strong> Courts ask why this person, out of the whole family, must be physically present — performing last rites, giving away a daughter, signing documents no one else can.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>A manageable risk profile.</strong> Antecedents, conduct in custody, behaviour on any earlier interim release. A clean record makes short release easy to supervise; a violent one makes every day of liberty a risk the court must own.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Honest duration.</strong> Ask for the days the occasion needs. Inflated requests signal that the occasion is a vehicle rather than a reason.</div>
</div>

<h2>Reading refusals correctly</h2>
<p>A refusal of occasion-based interim bail decides nothing about guilt and little about future applications; it decides only that this applicant, on this record, for this occasion, did not make the case. The jurisprudence keeps two values in balance. Prisoners do not forfeit family life — courts routinely release undertrials for funerals, weddings and medical emergencies, because humanity inside the system is part of what legitimises it. But the discretion is anchored in verification and necessity, precisely so that it survives misuse. Applications that respect that anchoring — specific, documented, modest — succeed even on difficult records; applications that treat the occasion as a formality fail even on easier ones.</p>
<div class="note">This note summarises an order of the Delhi High Court for general information. Whether interim bail is available in any matter depends on its own facts, record and the applicable rules.</div>
